# --- The machine ------------------------------------------------------------
# Two translation units, split by subject the way the compiler's four are: the
# instruction loop in Vm.cpp, and what a frame is in VmFrames.cpp -- how one is
# pushed, how a name finds a slot in it, how a throw takes one off, and what the
# collector sees through it. The loop reads all of that and none of it reads the
# loop.
# One entry per thing the machine can do, with its effect on the operand stack
# written beside it. That comment is the contract the compiler and the VM have
# with each other, so the lines here are description rather than surface: the
# member and method counts are what would say this is growing behaviour, and an
# enum has neither. It grows with the language and nothing else.
# Grew by three for generators, which is the language growing: Yield and
# GeneratorEntry are the two halves of suspending a generator, and
# IterateDelegate is the step `yield*` needs and no other loop does -- it is
# the only one that pushes the iterator's *return* value rather than dropping
# it.
# Grew by three more for `for await`. Two of them can end its loop, which is
# what the third line of each comment is for: a sync iterable answers "no more"
# when it is stepped and an async one answers inside the promise that step
# returned, so the branch is on either side of the Await between them.
# Grew by two more: ToStringOp, because a template substitution is ToString
# rather than whatever `+` would pick, and CopyScope, which is what makes a
# `let` in a loop head one binding per iteration rather than one for the loop.
# And by one for IterateForward, which is what turns `yield*` from a loop into
# a relationship: a throw or a forced return arriving at its `yield` goes to
# the delegate rather than stopping at the outer generator.
budget: Op              header_lines=255 public_methods=0  members=0
# One function's code and everything it names. Data: seven vectors, three
# scalars, and no behaviour. It grows when a chunk has to remember one more
# kind of thing, which has happened twice and should be argued for each time.
# Grew by three for slot resolution: the property keys a named access uses, the
# declaration records DeclareSlot reads, and the size of the function's own
# scope. All three are things the compiler worked out once so the machine does
# not have to work them out per execution, which is what this whole struct is.
# Grew by one for the flattened frame: whether this function's scopes live in
# the frame or on the heap. One bit, and it has to be here rather than derived
# at run time because the compiler resolved every name against the answer --
# a name in a flattened function is one hop count and a name in a scoped one is
# another, so the two disagreeing is not a slowdown, it is a wrong slot.
# Grew by one for async: whether calling this returns a promise and its body
# can suspend. On the code rather than on the function object, because it is a
# property of the *body* -- the machine reads it in PushFrame to make the
# promise and in Return to settle it, and both already have the code.
# Grew by one for generators, and it is the same question one bit over: what a
# call hands its caller. `is_async` says a promise, `is_generator` says an
# iterator, and the two are independent rather than a mode -- `async function*`
# is both, which is why they are not one enum. On the code for the same reason
# `is_async` is: PushFrame reads it to make the generator object, and the
# compiler reads it to decide whether the body gets an entry suspend.
# Grew by one for `call_names`: what each call instruction was calling, by
# name. It passes the test above -- it is something the compiler worked out
# once so the machine does not have to, and the machine *cannot*. "undefined is
# not a function" names the fault and not the place, and on minified script the
# frames are anonymous so a stack does not name it either. Sparse: only calls
# whose callee had a name.
# Grew by one member and one method for `positions`: the source offset each
# instruction was compiled from. It passes the same test `call_names` above
# does -- something the compiler worked out once that the machine cannot
# recover -- and it is what turned `ReferenceError: HS is not defined` from a
# scoping bug with no location into `at HS (@1814415)`, which named a named
# function expression neither engine bound. The method is the sparse lookup;
# it is on the struct because the table's shape is the struct's business and
# a caller doing its own binary search would be a second one to get wrong.
budget: CompiledFunction header_lines=135 public_methods=1 members=20
# One call in progress. Every field is something a resumed frame would need, and
# that is the test for adding another -- this is the record `await` will have to
# be able to copy somewhere and put back.
# Grew by one for the flattened frame: where this call's bindings start on the
# locals stack. Passes the test above -- a resumed frame has to know where its
# own bindings are before it can read one.
# Grew by one for async: the promise the call returns. This is the field that
# makes the record above worth being a record -- a filed frame is found again
# through the reaction attached to this, and settling it is what a return or a
# throw out of an async call actually does.
# Grew by one for generators, and it passes the test above: a resumed frame has
# to know which generator it is the body of before a `yield` can record where it
# filed itself. The promise finds its frame through a reaction; a generator's
# frame finds its generator through this, which is the same relationship read
# from the other end.
budget: Frame           header_lines=56 public_methods=0  members=12
# The compiler. Large because the language is, and split across four translation
# units by what each compiles rather than by size: the emitter and the
# expressions in Compiler.cpp, statements and patterns in
# CompilerStatements.cpp, scopes and slot resolution in CompilerScopes.cpp, and
# how a function body becomes a chunk in CompilerFunctions.cpp. The last of
# those came out of the first when generators pushed it past the cap, and it
# was the right seam rather than the nearest one: `Function` and its two helpers
# are not expressions, which is what Compiler.cpp says it holds.
# The member count is what matters and it is small -- three tables, three depths
# and two flags, all of them state that exists only while one function is being
# emitted.
# Grew by three for slot resolution: the enclosing function's compiler, the
# scopes this one has open, and how many of those are not block scopes. The
# three together are the model of the run-time scope chain, and it has to be a
# model rather than a lookup because the whole point is to answer "how many
# scopes out, which slot" without running anything.
# Grew by one member and one method for optional chains. Short-circuiting is a
# property of the *chain* rather than of the link -- `a?.b.c` with a nullish
# `a` is undefined for the whole expression -- so the exits are collected while
# the spine compiles and merged once at the end. The member is where they are
# collected, and it is suspended around a computed key and around call
# arguments, because an optional chain written inside one of those is its own.
budget: Compiler        header_lines=205 public_methods=5  members=16
