# Grew for classes: the home object `super` resolves against, the superclass,
# and the instance field initializers. All three are per-function state that
# has nowhere else to live -- a class *is* a function here, which is what keeps
# `new` from having to know which of the two forms it was handed.
# Grew by one for the frozen flag. It is a bool beside `marked_` rather than a
# property on the object, because a frozen object must refuse writes from a
# *builtin* too -- `arr.push` on a frozen array does not go through
# SetProperty -- and a property could be deleted by the page it is meant to
# constrain.
# Grew by one member and two methods for the compiled body. A function has
# either an AST body or a chunk and never both -- they are two spellings of the
# same thing, and which one a function got is what CallFunction dispatches on.
# The pointer is on the object rather than in a table beside the heap, which is
# where compiled patterns and the weak tables live, because this one is read on
# every call and a hash lookup per call is the one place that cost would show.
# Grew for the integrity levels replacing the frozen flag -- three nested
# states rather than one -- and for the symbol keys, which key_order_
# deliberately does not hold.
# And again for the property attributes. A property had none, so
# `Object.defineProperty(exports, '__esModule', {value: true})` -- which every
# bundler emits and which is *non*-enumerable -- landed in `Object.keys`. Three
# bools on Property, an enumerable-only key list beside the full one, and a
# Define that takes them; the member count did not move, because they are
# fields of a property rather than of an object.
# And once more for the typed arrays. One member: the window onto a buffer,
# set for an ArrayBuffer, a typed array and a DataView and null for everything
# else. A pointer rather than the record inline, because every other object
# would carry forty bytes for a kind of object a page makes a handful of -- and
# beside the heap rather than on the object is where the compiled patterns and
# the map indexes live, which is right for those and wrong for this: it is read
# on every element access and a hash lookup there would show.
# And by one for a bigint's digits, borrowed from the heap's table. A pointer
# on the cell rather than a lookup, because every arithmetic operation reads it
# twice and a hash there would be the cost of the whole type.
# +1 member and +5 lines for `serializable_`: whether the *language* may copy this object.
# It is a bool rather than a slot the binding layer sets, because `src/js` is the module that
# does the copying and `src/bindings` is the one that knows an object is a handle onto something
# outside this heap. A structured clone of a `URL` would otherwise be a plain object carrying its
# href with none of its behaviour -- which a page keeps until something calls a method on it.
