budget: Heap        header_lines=160 public_methods=26 members=11
# The tree-walking interpreter.
#
# The member count went *down* at the commit that raised these numbers, from
# nineteen to thirteen: the seven well-known values -- the five prototypes,
# Promise.prototype and the `Symbol.iterator` cell -- are one grouped member
# now rather than seven loose ones. That was the budget doing its job. The old
# shape kept the fields here and the root list in MaybeCollect, so adding one
# and forgetting the other was a use-after-free nothing would catch until a
# page allocated enough; `WellKnown::Roots()` is now the only list.
#
# The header is larger than before because that grouping is written out where
# it lives, along with the microtask record. Lines here are description; the
# member and method counts are what say whether this is becoming a god object,
# and both have room.
# The well-known values, grouped. One field per thing the language requires to
# exist and one `Roots()` that cannot disagree with the fields -- which is the
# whole reason it is a struct. It grows as the language does.
# Grew by one for %GeneratorPrototype%, which is the language growing: `next`,
# `throw`, `return` and the `Symbol.iterator` that hands back the generator
# itself. One object shared by every generator rather than one per generator
# function, which is the one place a page could tell and is not a place a page
# looks.
# Grew by two for async generators: their prototype, which shares no method
# with the sync one, and the `Symbol.asyncIterator` cell `for await` resolves
# against. Both are what the entry above calls the language growing.
# And by one for the return signal: the prototype of the value a forced return
# travels as. It is here rather than being a flag on the interpreter because
# two forced returns can be in flight at once -- a `finally` can make another
# generator return -- so the marker has to be on the value rather than beside
# the machine.
# Grew by three for the symbols an *operator* consults: `Symbol.toPrimitive`
# for `+`, `Symbol.hasInstance` for `instanceof`, and `Symbol.toStringTag` for
# `Object.prototype.toString`. Held here for the reason `Symbol.iterator` is --
# a page can assign to the global `Symbol`, and an operator may not stop
# working when it does.
# And by one for Boolean.prototype, which is the same category the string and
# number prototypes are: a boolean is a primitive here, so its methods live on
# a shared object GetProperty consults rather than on a wrapper.
# And by one for the marker a short-circuited optional chain travels as in the
# tree-walker. It is here rather than beside the interpreter for the reason
# return_signal is: it is a value, and a value that has to survive a collection
# is a root.
# And by two for the typed arrays: a typed array made without a buffer
# allocates one and has to give it the right prototype, and the nine
# constructors share the one prototype the generic methods live on.
budget: WellKnown   header_lines=145 public_methods=2  members=20
# Grew by one member for the machine: VmState groups the value stack, the frame
# stack, the block scopes, the open iteration cursors and the compiled programs.
# One member rather than five, for the reason WellKnown above is one rather than
# seven -- every field in it is a GC root, and a root list living apart from the
# fields is a use-after-free waiting for a page to allocate enough. GatherVmRoots
# sits next to the struct and walks all of it.
# Grew by one member for the calls that are waiting: Suspensions groups the
# filed frames and the counter that names them, for the reason WellKnown and
# VmState are each one member rather than several -- every value in there is a
# GC root, and a root list living apart from the fields is a use-after-free
# waiting for a page to allocate enough. GatherSuspensionRoots sits next to the
# struct and walks all of it.
#
# The header grew by more, because Suspension is written out where it lives:
# what a suspended call consists of is the substance of async/await, and the
# list is the same one the handler table records depths of.
#
# Grew again for generators, and by lines rather than by members or methods --
# which is the shape a well-factored addition has here. The member count did not
# move at all: a generator's state lives beside its object on the heap, next to
# the compiled patterns and the weak tables, so nothing new is held here. What
# grew is description -- the two halves a suspend was split into, and what each
# of `yield`, `next`, `throw` and `return` does to a filed frame.
#
# And again for async generators, and again by lines alone -- the member count
# has not moved through any of this. What a running async generator needs that
# nothing else does is a queue of unanswered requests, and that went into the
# Suspensions struct beside the filed frames rather than becoming a member of
# its own, because every promise in it is a GC root and the root walk for those
# already exists. Five methods and the record they pass around are the growth,
# and the record is written out where it lives for the reason Suspension is.
#
# Grew again for the conversions that can run script. `ToNumber` and `ToString`
# in Value.h are pure functions with no interpreter, so they answer NaN and
# "[object Object]" for every object -- which makes `[] + {}`, `+[]`, `[1] == 1`
# and `date - date` all quietly wrong. ToPrimitive and the four built on it are
# methods because they call a page's `valueOf`, and they return a Result because
# that call can throw. Five public methods and an enum, and they are public for
# the reason CallFunction is: the host converts values too, and a second
# implementation of `+` is the thing this whole file argues against.
# And by one member for `new.target`: what it is for the call about to be made.
# Set by Construct and taken by whichever call path binds the frame, so it is
# in effect for exactly one call. A member rather than an argument because both
# engines and four call paths reach the same place, and threading it through
# all of them would be four chances to forget.
# And by two members for modules: the host's resolver, and the table of
# records keyed by resolved specifier. The table is what makes a shared
# dependency shared rather than run twice, and is what a cycle is broken by.
# The resolver is a member because loading is the host's job -- a specifier's
# meaning is a URL question, and a language engine has no business answering
# one.
# Grew by one member for `constructing_`: the object each in-flight `new` is
# building, innermost last. It exists because `super()` may *replace* what is
# being constructed -- a base constructor that returns an object becomes the
# derived class's `this`, which the engine did not implement and which is the
# rule custom elements are built on. A stack rather than a field because
# constructors nest, and separate from `active_objects_` because that one is a
# root list shared with iteration cursors, so its back() is not reliably the
# thing being constructed.
# Raised by three lines for `ReportUncaught`, which is the channel an exception in an event listener
# reaches a page through -- see bindings/EventDispatch.cpp, where discarding it was silently losing
# whole scripts.
# And by three more for `InstallConsole`, which is a *file* moving out rather than
# surface arriving: `console` is the host's diagnostic channel and not part of the
# language, and Builtins.cpp going over the module cap is what said so.
# And by 29 more for `ValueRoot`, which is a use-after-free fix rather than a
# feature. A `js::Value` in a C++ local is invisible to the collector -- the
# rule this whole class is written around -- and the *completion value* was
# breaking it: RunCompiled took what a script threw, drained the microtask
# queue, and returned it, and draining is where MaybeCollect runs. A page whose
# script threw with enough allocation behind it read freed memory. The lines
# are the class and the reason; the member and method counts did not move,
# because it is a scope guard rather than state.
# And by 12 more for the stack on `ReportUncaught`. Not surface -- the signature
# is unchanged and no member moved -- but the *content* of a reported error,
# which is the difference between "a callback threw" and "a callback threw at
# offset 1323410". Every caller is a binding reporting a page's own failure, and
# those are the throws with the least context available anywhere else.
# And by 12 more for the OOM string path in the same method: when MakeError
# cannot allocate an Error object it falls back to a bare string, and that
# string still needs CaptureStack appended or youtube's eight heap_oom throws
# report with no place. See 94317dd and TD-0012.
# And by NestedHostBudget / ElementUpgradeBudget (lines): MSE and CE upgrades
# under live frames need a shared nested hang-guard refresh (TD-0018 / TD-0020).
# Member count unchanged (renamed media_* → nested_host_*); methods unchanged.
budget: Interpreter header_lines=1335 public_methods=70 members=29
# One module, as the engine sees it: what it is called, its tree, its own
# top-level scope, what it publishes, what it asked for, and how far it has
# got. Six fields and a status, and every one of them is something the
# evaluation order needs -- which is the test for adding a seventh.
# Grew by one, and the entry above asks for an argument. `source_length` is
# not evaluation-order state: it is the one fact about the source that the
# tree does not carry, and a module's body is compiled at *evaluation* time,
# by which point the source is gone. The compiler's instruction bound is a
# ratio against it -- see kInstructionsPerSourceByte -- so without this a
# module would be held to the floor no matter how large it was.
budget: Module      header_lines=40 public_methods=0  members=8
# The machine's state, grouped. Five vectors and nothing else: it is a bag of
# stacks by design, and anything that wants to be a *method* on it belongs on
# Interpreter, next to the property lookups the opcodes call.
# Grew by one for the locals stack: the bindings of every frame whose scopes
# nothing can capture, one contiguous slice per frame. A sixth stack rather
# than a vector per frame, for the reason the other five are shared -- a vector
# per frame is a heap allocation per call, which is the cost this whole change
# exists to remove. It is a GC root like the rest, and GatherVmRoots walks it.
budget: VmState     header_lines=36 public_methods=0  members=6
# Why the bytecode compiler gave up on a program, when it does. An enum rather
# than a bool because a bailout is otherwise invisible -- the program still runs,
# on the tree-walker, which refuses an async function *at the call*, so the
# visible failure is a TypeError in an unrelated function. Two of these reasons
# are bounds and three are defects, and telling them apart is the point: it is
# what found the destructuring-default bug that made reddit's whole bundle fall
# back. See `MICROBROWSER_PERF_COUNTERS=1` and `js.compile_bailout_*`.
budget: BailoutReason header_lines=35 public_methods=0 members=0
